  "content": {
    "title": "How to survive (and thrive) at Coachella 2026",
    "text": "Each year, Coachella tickets sell out months in advance, drawing crowds of over 180,000, sometimes before a lineup is even announced. This year is the festival's 25th anniversary, and the headliners are Sabrina Carpenter, Justin Bieber, and Karol G. All three are making their Coachella headlining debuts, and Karol G will make history as the first Latina artist to top the bill. The festival runs April 10–12 and April 17–19, 2026, in Indio, California and if you haven’t been before, it’s not just about pulling up to a concert. It's a ton of planning and dollars: securing tickets, booking flights, locking down accommodations (many camp on-site, where silent discos run through the night), early wake-ups, and most importantly…the outfits.As someone who’s done Coachella before, here are my tips for how to survive and thrive at Coachella this year…How to survive (and thrive) at Coachella(James Barrett @jimmyrox)Go the extra mile for the port-a-potties.There will be a sea of port-a-johns, and yes, most are disgusting. Have you ever seen an overflowing toilet during Beyoncé's set? The key here is to trek all the way (far away) to the back of the sea of port-a-johns where you'll be rewarded with a cleaner stall since many people just use one of the first ones they see.Also, a luxury of VIP tickets are much, much fancier bathrooms.Go old school with walkie talkiesWith over 180,000 people in the desert, chances are that you'll lose cell phone reception when you're on Coachella grounds. I'm taking it back to middle school here to tell you that walkie talkies will help you and your buddies out greatly. This small device will save you a headache of getting lost and missing out on screaming ‘House Tour’ with your friends during Sabrina Carpenter’s set.Also, who doesn't love a walkie talkie, over!Know who you want to see for the day ahead of timeArtist times stay on schedule so don't be late. There are six major stages, so between you and your friends you should have a game plan of who you want to see and when. Definitely check out some new artists and if your friends don't want to see them, go solo. You'll probably make new friends along the way who will match your vibes.The main stage view is sometimes better from general admission vs. VIPVIP definitely has its perks, but for viewing the main stage my hot take is that the general admission area had a better view. When I saw Beyoncé, the VIP section was insanely packed. There is a level above VIP called Artist Pass which gets you even closer, but you can't purchase them. Artist Passes are those invited by artists playing.Anything but your average festival foodFestival food used to mean a sad hot dog and a warm beer. Not here. Coachella features more than 100 restaurants, bars, and pop-ups across the grounds, and the spread has evolved into something you might actually plan your day around.Most meals will run you $15–$25 or more, with drinks even costlier, so budget accordingly. The hub for general admission attendees is the Indio Central Market, a shaded food hall where you can eat, reset, and even hear the Outdoor Stage acts while you sit. For those going higher-end, the 12 Peaks VIP area is your culinary playground, with chef-driven concepts like Le Burger by Camphor and truffle-loaded comfort food from Truffle Boys. Nobu has taken over the Red Bull Mirage with a reservation-only omakase experience–think signature yellowtail jalapeño and premium nigiri, overlooking the Quasar Stage. For an escape from the crowds, head to the VIP Rose Garden if your ticket has access.There’s plenty more to see besides the music artistsCoachella isn't all about the music–the on-grounds experience has leveled up considerably.On the art side, new commissions this year include \"Visage Brut,\" a steel totem featuring anthropomorphic characters by The Los Angeles Design Group, and Sabine Marcelis' desert mirage-inspired installation called \"Maze.\" And if you've been before, you'll recognize \"Spectra,” the rainbow tower that first arrived in 2018 and never actually left.For brand activations, Wavytalk is offering complimentary hairstyling on-site, with professional stylists doing everything from bubble braids to jeweled strands and bouncy blowouts. Aperol has debuted a brand-new Day Club with live DJ sets, interactive beauty stations featuring tooth gems and permanent bracelets, with surprise guest appearances.HBO will also be premiering Euphoria Season 3 on the campgrounds.American Express is back and still one of the best reasons to carry your card into the desert. All cardmembers get access to the on-site Amex Experience–in GA, between the Mojave and Gobi stages, where you can bring up to three guests. Inside you'll find a dedicated Merch To-Go window with exclusive festival bundles. There's also a dedicated cardmember merch lane at the Festival Store and Artist Store so you can skip the lines. Gap is showing up in a big way…the Hoodie House lets you purchase and personalize an exclusive Gap x Coachella hoodie with custom patches, beads, and daily charm drops. It's the successor to the original embroidery booth, basically.Have a hoodie for when the sun goes downA good reason to buy Coachella merch. This one sounds obvious until you're standing at the main stage at midnight wondering why you're shivering in a crop top. Coachella Valley is a desert, and deserts don't play fair with temperature. Daytime temperatures push past 100 degrees, then drop to 55 or 60 at night. Don’t get freezing in the desert ruin your night.Always pace yourselfPlease be responsible. I don't care if I sound like your mom.Coachella is different because you are literally in a desert, so hydrate, don't black out before Justin Bieber comes on and of course…have fun!",
